 explain implication.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate C difficulty communicating ideas, keeps calm, makes correct decisions quickly, understands complicated technology. Also we have no info on B's memory weakness for numbers, and nasty remarks. We have also notes on A and D.\n\nWe can either agree with Sarah, or challenge. Let's consider: B's uncooperative behavior (makes nasty remarks) is a serious crew cooperation issue. C's communication difficulty also serious. D has hot temper and know-it-all, also crew cooperation issues. A is show-off, not open to new ideas, but has leadership and anticipation.\n\nWe might argue that B's uncooperative behavior is more severe than C's communication difficulty? Or that C's reliability and decision speed may outweigh communication difficulty? But communication is critical. However we could argue that B's memory weakness for numbers could affect attention/accuracy, which is also critical. That might tip away from B.\n\nWe could propose Candidate D? D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude may affect crew cooperation. No info on reliability.\n\nWe could propose Candidate A? A has leadership, anticipation, spatial vision, but show-off, not open to new ideas, no info on stress handling.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed A and D, we could bring them up. Perhaps we can argue that we need more info on A and D before final decision, and we are not convinced B is best yet. So we could change vote to \"undecided\" but must vote for a candidate. The instruction says vote must be one of the candidates. We could vote for Candidate D as a provisional, arguing that D's strengths (responsibility, problem solving, concentration) and we need to explore crew cooperation concerns.\n\nBut we need to base on explicit facts: D responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: know-it-all, hot temper. B's concerns: nasty remarks, weak memory for numbers. C's concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. A's concerns: show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nWhich candidate overall? Hard. Perhaps we can argue that D's strengths in attention, problem solving, responsibility outweigh his hot temper, and that his hot temper may be less severe than B's nasty remarks. Also D's memory not mentioned as weak. So we could shift vote to D.\n\nWe need to reference Sarah's point about communication deficit of C, and B's uncooperative behavior. We can say while B's strengths are strong, his nasty remarks and weak memory for numbers raise concerns about crew cooperation and attention, making D a more balanced candidate.\n\nThus we propose vote for Candidate D.\n\nLet's craft PUBLIC_MESSAGE: mention Sarah's argument, note B's strengths but also note B's weak memory for numbers (attention) and nasty remarks (crew cooperation). Compare with D's strengths (responsibility, problem solving, concentration) and note concerns but perhaps manageable. Conclude we now recommend D.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: B makes nasty remarks about his colleagues; B has relatively weak memory for numbers. D concentrates very well; D solves problems extremely well; D takes responsibility seriously; D responds to unexpected events adequately. Also D is regarded as a know-it-all and has a hot temper (concerns). Use these.\n\nNow produce output."}
