events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence, decision quality), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), regarded as a know-it-all (cooperation negative), has a hot temper (cooperation negative), considered moody, regarded as a loner (cooperation negative). No explicit reliability.\n\nNow we need to decide which candidate best fits. B has strong reliability and stress handling but crew cooperation issues. D has strong problem solving, responsibility, attention, but also cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper, loner). C has reliability (maybe 100% reliable? Not in Emily's notes but James said 100% reliable? Actually not in Emily's notes; but James earlier said C is 100% reliable? Not in given notes; but we have \"Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly\" and \"Candidate C has excellent attention skills\". Also \"Candidate C puts concern for others above everything\" suggests good crew cooperation. But also difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric. Mixed.\n\nA has leadership, anticipation, spatial vision, but unfriendly, eats unhealthily (maybe reliability concerns). No info on stress.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed A yet, Emily could bring A into discussion. She could argue that A's leadership and anticipation are valuable, but unfriendly and unhealthy habits raise concerns about crew cooperation and reliability. Might not be best.\n\nShe could argue that despite B's crew cooperation issues, his reliability and stress handling are critical, and his memory weakness for numbers is a concern but could be mitigated. D's cooperation issues also severe. C's communication difficulty is also severe. So B might still be best despite issues.\n\nAlternatively, she could argue that D's strengths in attention, problem solving, responsibility outweigh his cooperation issues, and that B's memory weakness is more critical for safety (attention). So D might be better.\n\nWe need to decide a vote. Let's weigh: For long-distance pilot, attention and accuracy are crucial. B's weak memory for numbers could be dangerous. D has strong concentration and problem solving, no memory issue noted. D's cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper) could affect crew resource management, but perhaps less fatal than memory errors. Also D takes responsibility seriously, which is good. So D might be the best overall.\n\nEmily could argue that D's strengths in attention, problem solving, responsibility, and stress handling (responds to unexpected events) outweigh his cooperation concerns, which are not directly observed but can be mitigated with training. B's memory weakness is a concrete safety risk. So she votes for D.\n\nShe should reference James's earlier recommendation for D, and the new evidence from Sarah about B's crew cooperation issues, and James's note about B's weak memory for numbers. Also note D's strengths from her own notes.\n\nThus Emily's public message: \"I agree with James's recommendation for D, because B's weak memory for numbers and uncooperative behavior are serious, while D's strong concentration, problem solving, responsibility, and adequate response to unexpected events make him a more reliable choice. Although D is known as a know-it-all and has a hot temper, these can be mitigated, and there is no evidence of memory deficits. Therefore I vote for Candidate D.\"\n\nLet's produce that."}
